WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.857
Level 0 represents the physical process itself.

00:00:03.857 --> 00:00:18.150
It includes the equipment and conditions being measured or influenced: material moving through a line, water in a treatment stage, power flowing through a substation, or temperature inside a vessel.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.460
This is where digital decisions become physical outcomes.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:29.493
Sensors and instrumentation report process conditions such as pressure, flow, speed, position, and temperature.

00:00:29.493 --> 00:00:31.989
Actuators create physical change.

00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:37.207
They open valves, start motors, move mechanisms, or operate breakers.

00:00:37.207 --> 00:00:44.693
A false measurement or incorrect actuation can affect product quality, equipment, safety, and the environment.

00:00:44.693 --> 00:00:47.643
Level 1 provides basic control.

00:00:47.643 --> 00:00:57.398
Programmable logic controllers, remote terminal units, distributed control components, and other controllers read inputs, apply control logic, and update outputs.

00:00:57.398 --> 00:01:02.616
Many perform this work continuously and within strict timing requirements.

00:01:02.616 --> 00:01:07.380
Their configuration, logic, and communication relationships are operationally sensitive.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.145
Security at these levels must respect safety and reliability.

00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:19.178
Aggressive scanning, untested endpoint software, or an unexpected restart can interfere with deterministic control.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:30.294
Protection often relies on physical safeguards, restricted access, network isolation, authenticated engineering workflows, configuration backups, change control, and monitoring at nearby network points.

00:01:30.294 --> 00:01:40.503
Level 0 and Level 1 also remind analysts that a device's consequence cannot be inferred from its size or operating system.

00:01:40.503 --> 00:01:44.814
A small controller may influence a critical process.

00:01:44.814 --> 00:01:57.746
Risk assessment should identify what the device controls, what independent safety functions exist, how loss of communication affects the process, and how trusted operation can be restored.
